<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Rhegion</h1><p class="st-meta">720 BCE – 270 BCE (approx.) · capital Rhegion</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city on the toe of Italy guarding the Strait of Messina, which under the tyrant Anaxilas controlled both shores of the strait.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Chalcidians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, joined by exiles from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/messene">Messenia</a>, founded Rhegion around 720 BCE at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, on the Delphic oracle's advice according to tradition. Facing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Zancle</a> across the Strait, it controlled shipping between the Tyrrhenian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Seas</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Rhegion adopted the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charondas">Charondas</a> and was ruled by an aristocracy of Messenian descent until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaxilas">Anaxilas</a> made himself tyrant around 494 BCE. He seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zancle">Zancle</a>, renamed it <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamertine-messana">Messana</a>, and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. The city was later a democracy and an ally of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>. The lyric poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibycus">Ibycus</a> and the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a> came from Rhegion. Its coins showing a lion's head and a hare are well known.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysius-i-of-syracuse">Dionysius I</a> captured and destroyed Rhegion in 387 BCE, but it revived. In 280 BCE, during the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a>, a Roman garrison of Campanian troops seized the city; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> retook it in 270 BCE and executed the mutineers. It remained a Greek-speaking ally, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a> Iulium, and is today Reggio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/rhegion.jpg" alt="Rhegion" width="320" height="218"><figcaption>Image: Mimmo Zema · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reggio_calabria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>720 BCE – 270 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rhegion</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Anaxilas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rhegium, Reggio Calabria (ancient)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-495&amp;focus=rhegion" aria-label="Rhegion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5070 808 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rhegion lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5390 1064L5383 1099M5390 1064L5304 942M5390 1064L5387 1061"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 1099h0M5304 942h0M5387 1061h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5390 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5390 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-495&amp;focus=rhegion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

